WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:06.649
Zipping through the streets.

00:00:06.649 --> 00:00:08.740
Blocking the sidewalks.

00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:15.280
Dockless electric scooters have appeared virtually
overnight in dozens of cities.

00:00:15.290 --> 00:00:20.340
So far this year, millions of rides were taken
on little machines just like this.

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:24.330
And their operators have raised billions in
investments.

00:00:24.330 --> 00:00:30.439
So if you haven’t yet, chances are you're going to
see stuff like this pretty soon.

00:00:30.439 --> 00:00:33.490
We see the scooters navigating through pedestrians.

00:00:33.490 --> 00:00:36.140
We see them being left anywhere.

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:40.500
And this creates problems for a number of
people.

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:42.140
Problems indeed.

00:00:42.220 --> 00:00:46.980
If people on dockless scooters or bikes keep
using the sidewalks … it’s going to get

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:47.980
crowded.

00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:58.420
the purpose of the sidewalk has changed considerably
over the last 100 years.

00:00:58.420 --> 00:01:04.930
when cities first started building sidewalks,
the reason for them was to accommodate pedestrians

00:01:04.930 --> 00:01:05.930
on the street.

00:01:05.930 --> 00:01:15.890
But this was not the only use of sidewalks.

00:01:15.890 --> 00:01:19.180
A lot of that happens before the automobile.

00:01:19.180 --> 00:01:24.369
Here’s a street scene from San Francisco
in 1906.

00:01:24.369 --> 00:01:29.100
Notice the man, holding a baby, just, walking
straight through traffic.

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:35.270
Nobody seems too concerned. they are quite
relaxed about it and they also know how to

00:01:35.270 --> 00:01:36.590
navigate the street.

00:01:39.590 --> 00:01:40.909
But, eventually cars got bigger and faster.

00:01:40.909 --> 00:01:42.659
Traffic had to separate.

00:01:42.659 --> 00:01:48.299
We start seeing with the proliferation
of the cars, many many cities start widening

00:01:48.299 --> 00:01:49.299
their streets.

00:01:49.299 --> 00:01:52.789
and of course this happens at the expense
of the sidewalk because a lot of times the

00:01:52.789 --> 00:01:59.080
buildings were pretty much set.

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:01.820
So a sidewalk that was 10 — 12 feet becomes 6 — 8 feet.

00:02:01.820 --> 00:02:04.549
Which is pretty unfair for pedestrians.

00:02:04.549 --> 00:02:07.310
Because there is a lot more happening on sidewalks
than walking.

00:02:07.310 --> 00:02:10.219
There’s the frontage zone

00:02:10.219 --> 00:02:14.220
— in cities, a business might put a cafe
or signs here.

00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:23.940
Then there’s the furniture zone, full of 
streetlights, newspaper racks, and benches.

00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:27.900
Because sidewalks have frontage and furniture zones, there's less space to travel than it might seem.

00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:32.470
Some vigilantes have taken the space issue into their own hands

00:02:32.470 --> 00:02:35.800
And many cities have used pilot programs to really get the scooters

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:40.250
under control.These pilots are likely to extend.

00:02:40.250 --> 00:02:43.770
After all, cities need alternative modes of
transportation.

00:02:43.770 --> 00:02:50.730
First it takes vehicles off the street
Because people who might be using ride hail,

00:02:50.730 --> 00:02:54.870
or taxis, or another personal vehicle, might
instead opt for 

00:02:54.870 --> 00:02:56.760
this other solution

00:02:56.760 --> 00:03:04.980
if a person takes a subway or a bus to a certain
station or stop, and they still live a mile

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:09.260
away from that station, they have to figure
out how to get there

00:03:09.260 --> 00:03:13.390
Even in cities with exceptional levels of
public transportation, many residents have

00:03:13.390 --> 00:03:15.560
to travel a ‘last mile’

00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:20.910
If dockless vehicles are deployed in areas
like this, residents could have more options

00:03:20.910 --> 00:03:21.910
for transportation.

00:03:21.910 --> 00:03:24.460
And not just rely on cars.

00:03:24.460 --> 00:03:28.480
Because what many people will do is say
‘I don’t have a last mile option that

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:29.600
works for me.

00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:32.610
I’m just going to drive the whole way.

00:03:32.610 --> 00:03:36.480
The cash influx for scooter-share is a really
a bet.

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:40.980
Investors hope that e-scooters can capture
demand in underserved transportation deserts,

00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:42.090
without adding congestion

00:03:42.090 --> 00:03:45.320
And the scooters might pull that off.

00:03:45.320 --> 00:03:49.610
One survey found robust support for e-scooters
as a substitute for short driving trips, or

00:03:49.610 --> 00:03:50.610
as a complement to public transit.

00:03:50.610 --> 00:03:54.750
But the scooters won’t work if their riders
have to compete for space with pedestrians

00:03:54.750 --> 00:03:56.110
and cars.

00:03:56.110 --> 00:04:02.680
we’re reaching a point in cities across
america, where it’s time to get people out

00:04:02.680 --> 00:04:06.950
of their cars and allow people more modes.

00:04:06.950 --> 00:04:13.740
Smaller transportation — bikes, segways,
scooters — they only work when cities make

00:04:13.740 --> 00:04:15.520
space for them.

00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:17.350
Planners can do this by designing ‘complete
streets’.

00:04:17.350 --> 00:04:23.080
So complete streets is a new term that entered
the lexicon of planning and transportation

00:04:23.080 --> 00:04:24.320
planning relatively recently.

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:31.270
but basically it is inspired by earlier streets
where you used to have all these different

00:04:31.270 --> 00:04:40.240
uses social uses of the streets and sidewalks
not only vehicular users

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:43.650
Complete streets start by reducing the amount
of space given to cars.

00:04:43.650 --> 00:04:51.780
Making space for protected bus bulbs, wider
sidewalks, street level plazas, and buffered

00:04:51.780 --> 00:04:54.000
bike lanes.

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:58.530
Scooters will be a more realistic last mile
option when cities build networks of complete

00:04:58.530 --> 00:04:59.730
streets.

00:04:59.730 --> 00:05:05.560
So, worry not, dear pedestrians — even if
the scooters are here to stay…

00:05:05.560 --> 00:05:10.380
Cities are drafting standards for this brave
new world of alternative transportation.

00:05:10.380 --> 00:05:15.650
With any luck, the love for scooters just
might push cities to invest in safer, more

00:05:15.650 --> 00:05:18.500
accomodating streets.

00:05:18.500 --> 00:05:25.530
Hi, thanks for watching and a special thanks
to the University of California for hitting

00:05:25.530 --> 00:05:26.650
the streets to help us make this episode.

